.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception
.Dd October 28, 2026
.Dt NQENC 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nqenc ,
.Nm nqencd ,
.Nm nqdec ,
.Nm nqkkg ,
.Nm nqkkgd ,
.Nm nqksize ,
.Nm nqkname ,
.Nm nqkfind
.Nd ML-KEM and HQC key encapsulation
.Sh LIBRARY
libnoqtis, \-lnoqtis
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In noqtis.h
.Ft int
.Fo nqkkg
.Fa "nqkalg alg"
.Fa "uint8_t *pk" "size_t pklen"
.Fa "uint8_t *sk" "size_t sklen"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo nqkkgd
.Fa "nqkalg alg"
.Fa "const uint8_t *seed" "size_t seedlen"
.Fa "uint8_t *pk" "size_t pklen"
.Fa "uint8_t *sk" "size_t sklen"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo nqenc
.Fa "nqkalg alg"
.Fa "const uint8_t *pk" "size_t pklen"
.Fa "uint8_t *ct" "size_t ctlen"
.Fa "uint8_t *ss" "size_t sslen"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo nqencd
.Fa "nqkalg alg"
.Fa "const uint8_t *pk" "size_t pklen"
.Fa "const uint8_t *seed" "size_t seedlen"
.Fa "uint8_t *ct" "size_t ctlen"
.Fa "uint8_t *ss" "size_t sslen"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo nqdec
.Fa "nqkalg alg"
.Fa "const uint8_t *sk" "size_t sklen"
.Fa "const uint8_t *ct" "size_t ctlen"
.Fa "uint8_t *ss" "size_t sslen"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fn nqksize "nqkalg alg" "nqksz *sz"
.Ft const char *
.Fn nqkname "nqkalg alg"
.Ft int
.Fn nqkfind "const char *name" "nqkalg *alg"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A key encapsulation mechanism lets anyone with a public key make a
ciphertext and a 32-byte shared secret, which only the holder of the
secret key can recover from the ciphertext.
noqtis provides ML-KEM (FIPS 203) and HQC (the HQC specification,
version 5.1 of 2026-11-09).
HQC is experimental: NIST selected it in March 2025 but its standard is
not final, and the final one may differ in its encoding; keys,
ciphertexts and secrets made now may then not interoperate.
Keys and ciphertexts of the earlier version 5.0 do not interoperate
with those of 5.1, and noqtis no longer reads them: see
.Sx CAVEATS .
Use ML-KEM for anything that must last.
.Pp
.Vt nqkalg
names the parameter set:
.Bl -column "NQMLKEM1024" "ML-KEM-1024" "pk" "sk" "ciphertext" "ss" -offset indent
.It Sy constant Ta Sy name Ta Sy pk Ta Sy sk Ta Sy ciphertext Ta Sy ss
.It Dv NQMLKEM512 Ta ML-KEM-512 Ta 800 Ta 1632 Ta 768 Ta 32
.It Dv NQMLKEM768 Ta ML-KEM-768 Ta 1184 Ta 2400 Ta 1088 Ta 32
.It Dv NQMLKEM1024 Ta ML-KEM-1024 Ta 1568 Ta 3168 Ta 1568 Ta 32
.It Dv NQHQC128 Ta HQC-128 Ta 2241 Ta 32 Ta 4433 Ta 32
.It Dv NQHQC192 Ta HQC-192 Ta 4514 Ta 32 Ta 8978 Ta 32
.It Dv NQHQC256 Ta HQC-256 Ta 7237 Ta 32 Ta 14421 Ta 32
.El
.Pp
.Fn nqksize
stores the sizes in
.Fa *sz ,
an
.Vt nqksz
with the members
.Va pk ,
.Va sk ,
.Va ct
and
.Va ss .
.Fn nqkname
returns the name, or
.Dv NULL
for an unknown value;
.Fn nqkfind
finds the algorithm whose name is exactly
.Fa name .
.Pp
.Fn nqkkg
makes a key pair from the operating system's random source.
.Fn nqkkgd
makes it from the standard's seed: 64 bytes (d || z) for ML-KEM, 32 bytes
for HQC.
An ML-KEM secret key holds the public key:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sk = dk_PKE || pk || H(pk) || z            (FIPS 203, Algorithm 16)
.Ed
.Pp
with H SHA3-256, dk_PKE 384k bytes and k 2, 3 or 4.
An HQC secret key is the 32-byte seed itself (dk = seed_KEM, as
specification 5.1 makes it by default): any 32 bytes are a key, and
.Fn nqkkgd
with a key as its seed gives the same key pair again.
.Pp
.Fn nqenc
encapsulates to the public key
.Fa pk :
it writes the ciphertext to
.Fa ct
and the shared secret to
.Fa ss .
.Fn nqencd
takes the encapsulation randomness from
.Fa seed
instead, for test vectors: the 32-byte m for ML-KEM, and m || salt for
HQC (32, 40 or 48 bytes for HQC-128, -192, -256).
For ML-KEM the public key is checked as FIPS 203 section 7.2 requires
(every coefficient below q = 3329); for HQC, the bits of its last byte
past the length n of the vector must be zero.
.Pp
.Fn nqdec
recovers the shared secret from
.Fa ct
with the secret key
.Fa sk ;
for ML-KEM the key is first checked as section 7.3 requires (the
H(pk) it holds must equal the hash of the pk it holds); for HQC the key
pair is first derived again from the seed.
A ciphertext of the right length that was not made for this key, or was
changed, gives a pseudorandom secret unrelated to the sender's and
returns
.Dv NQOK
(implicit rejection, as FIPS 203 and the HQC specification require).
An HQC secret key that is not 32 bytes, among them one made for
specification 5.0 (2321, 4602 or 7333 bytes), gives
.Dv NQEDEC .
It fails only on malformed sizes, on a secret key that fails that check
.Pq Dv NQEDEC ,
and, as both decapsulations are masked with fresh randomness, when the
operating system's random source fails
.Pq Dv NQERNG .
Protocols must therefore confirm the secret, for example by using it in an
authenticated cipher.
.Pp
Output buffers may be larger than the algorithm's sizes; only the defined
number of bytes is written.
Keys and ciphertexts given as input must have exactly the algorithm's
sizes.
The seed of
.Fn nqkkgd
may overlap
.Fa pk
or
.Fa sk ;
on any error the outputs are cleared.
.Ss In symbols
ML-KEM (FIPS 203, Algorithms 16 to 18), with H = SHA3-256,
G = SHA3-512, J(x) = SHAKE256(x, 32 bytes) and
.Sy ||
concatenation:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
KeyGen(d, z):   (ek, dk_PKE) = K-PKE.KeyGen(d)
                dk = dk_PKE || ek || H(ek) || z
Encaps(ek; m):  (K, r) = G(m || H(ek))
                c = K-PKE.Encrypt(ek, m, r)        ss = K
Decaps(dk, c):  m' = K-PKE.Decrypt(dk_PKE, c)
                (K', r') = G(m' || h)               h = H(ek) from dk
                Kbar = J(z || c)
                ss = K'    if K-PKE.Encrypt(ek, m', r') = c
                ss = Kbar  otherwise     (compared in constant time)
.Ed
.Pp
HQC (specification 5.1), with H(x) = SHA3-256(x || 0x01),
G(x) = SHA3-512(x || 0x00), I(x) = SHA3-512(x || 0x02),
J(x) = SHA3-256(x || 0x03), XOF SHAKE256, k = 16, 24 or 32 the message
length in bytes, + addition in GF(2)[X]/(X^n - 1), C the concatenated
Reed-Muller and Reed-Solomon code, SFW$ the fixed-weight sampler by
rejection and SFW Sendrier's fixed-weight sampler:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
KeyGen(dk):     seed_PKE || sigma = XOF(dk || byte(k), 32 + k)
                seed_dk || seed_ek = I(seed_PKE)
                y, x = SFW$(XOF(seed_dk), w), each of weight w
                h = XOF(seed_ek);  s = x + h y
                ek = seed_ek || s                  dk = the 32-byte seed
Encaps(ek; m, salt):
                K || theta = G(H(ek) || m || salt)
                r2, e, r1 = SFW(XOF(theta)), of weights w_r, w_e, w_r
                u = r1 + h r2
                v = C(m) + Truncate(s r2 + e)
                c = u || v || salt                 ss = K (32 bytes)
Decaps(dk, c):  (ek, y, sigma) = KeyGen(dk)
                m' = C.Decode(v - Truncate(u y))
                K' || theta' = G(H(ek) || m' || salt)
                Kbar = J(H(ek) || sigma || c)
                ss = K'    if Encaps(ek; m', salt) gives c
                ss = Kbar  otherwise     (compared in constant time)
.Ed
.Pp
Both are key encapsulation mechanisms built with a Fujisaki-Okamoto
transform with implicit rejection, meant to be IND-CCA2 secure: ML-KEM
under the module learning-with-errors assumption (FIPS 203 and its
references), HQC under the quasi-cyclic syndrome decoding assumptions of
its specification.
Those arguments are in the standards and the papers they cite, not in
this repository.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
Every function except
.Fn nqkname
returns
.Dv NQOK
(0) or a negative error code:
.Bl -tag -width NQENOSUP
.It Bq Er NQEARG
.Fa alg
is not an
.Vt nqkalg ,
a pointer is
.Dv NULL ,
or a
.Fa seed
does not have the standard's length.
.It Bq Er NQEBUF
An output buffer is smaller than the algorithm's size.
.It Bq Er NQEDEC
An input key or ciphertext does not have the algorithm's length, or a
key fails the standard's input check.
.It Bq Er NQERNG
The operating system's random source failed
.Po
.Fn nqkkg ,
.Fn nqenc ,
and
.Fn nqdec ,
whose masked decapsulation draws fresh mask shares
.Pc .
.It Bq Er NQENOSUP
.Fn nqkfind :
no algorithm has that name.
.El
.Pp
On any failure, the output buffers are zeroed.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The program
.Pa docs/examples/kem.c
of the source tree, with the sizes taken at run time:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nqksz z;
uint8_t *pk, *sk, *ct, a[32], b[32];
int rc = nqksize(NQMLKEM768, &z), same = 0;
if (rc != NQOK || z.ss != sizeof a)
  return 2;
pk = malloc(z.pk);
sk = malloc(z.sk);
ct = malloc(z.ct);
if (pk == NULL || sk == NULL || ct == NULL)
  return 2;
rc = nqkkg(NQMLKEM768, pk, z.pk, sk, z.sk);
if (rc == NQOK)
  rc = nqenc(NQMLKEM768, pk, z.pk, ct, z.ct, a, sizeof a);
if (rc == NQOK)
  rc = nqdec(NQMLKEM768, sk, z.sk, ct, z.ct, b, sizeof b);
if (rc == NQOK)
  same = nqcteq(a, b, sizeof a);
nqwipe(sk, z.sk);
nqwipe(a, sizeof a);
nqwipe(b, sizeof b);
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr noqtis-dec 1 ,
.Xr noqtis-enc 1 ,
.Xr noqtis 3 ,
.Xr nqcteq 3 ,
.Xr nqwipe 3
.Pp
The arithmetic of ML-KEM (the reductions, Compress and Decompress and
their error bound, the NTT moduli, ByteEncode and ByteDecode) and HQC's
GF(2^8) are proved in Lean in
.Pa proofs/lean
of the source tree
.Pq Pa Mlkem.lean , Ntt.lean , Enc.lean , Gf.lean ;
that the C computes those models is checked by tests, not proved.
.Sh STANDARDS
.Rs
.%T FIPS 203: Module-Lattice-Based Key-Encapsulation Mechanism Standard
.%Q NIST
.%D August 13, 2024
.Re
.Rs
.%A P. Gaborit
.%A C. Aguilar-Melchor
.%A N. Aragon
.%A others
.%T Hamming Quasi-Cyclic (HQC), specification, version 5.1
.%U https://pqc-hqc.org/doc/hqc_specifications_v5.1.pdf
.%D November 9, 2026
.Re
.Sh CAVEATS
noqtis 0.3.0 follows HQC specification 5.1; earlier releases followed
5.0, whose keys, ciphertexts and shared secrets differ.
A 5.0 secret key has another length and is refused
.Pq Dv NQEDEC .
A 5.0 public key or ciphertext has the same length as a 5.1 one and
cannot be told apart: encapsulating to a 5.0 public key gives a
ciphertext and secret that a 5.0 implementation does not share, and a
5.0 ciphertext decapsulates, by implicit rejection, to an unrelated
secret.
Make new HQC key pairs.
.Sh SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
Key generation, encapsulation and decapsulation run in constant time with
respect to the seeds, the randomness, the secret key and the shared
secret; both decapsulations are also first-order masked.
In HQC, a secret-derived bit becomes public in the one place its
specification allows (whether a draw of the key-generation sampler is
kept), and nowhere else.
Because an HQC secret key is only its seed, HQC decapsulation first
derives the key pair again; that step is constant time but not masked,
and only the parts the masked decryption uses are split into shares.
